// model one interface_id maps to a set of endpoint candidates, and Resolve couldn't tell which side
// to route to. The naming follows the camera / camera.config shape: two faces of the same domain.
//
// # What gap it fills
//
// USER_CONSENT permissions (perm.camera.capture and the like) can get into GrantedPermissions at install time,
// but at run time AllowedAt requires GrantState == GRANTED, which defaults to NOT_REQUESTED. In other words,
// once an ordinary app is installed, the sensitive permissions it requested can **never** be obtained: the app can't help itself (the SDK has no
// API for requesting permissions), and the user has no UI to approve them.
//
// This interface fills that question in at install: before installing, it lays out the sensitive permissions the package requests for the user, and
// whichever the user agrees to are stored directly as GRANTED once the install succeeds.
//
// # Why confirmation and installation must happen in the same call
//
// This interface **doesn't return the fact "the user agreed"** for the caller to install by itself. If it did, the caller could
// skip ConfirmInstall entirely and call pkgmanager's INSTALL directly, and the confirmation dialog would be pure decoration.
//
// As it is: only permissionui holds perm.permission.admin, and nervud's
// needs_user_confirmation gate only lets holders of that permission through (internal/protocheck). So
// installing over IPC **must** go through this interface; it's the only holder of the key to that gate. What the caller gets
// is the install's final result, and the confirmation in between can't be bypassed.
//
// # Boundary: this interface's implementer makes no security decisions
//
// The same division of labour as pkgmanager. Signatures, digests, ABI, upgrade decisions and the permission intersection are all in nervud's
// pkgregistry; permissionui does only two things: shows the permission text the kernel provides to the user,
// and passes the user's choices on together with the install request. It doesn't even decide whether a permission is USER_CONSENT:
// nervud filters that by the Catalog definitions when storing (out-of-bounds entries are silently ignored).
